WHEN WORK MOVES INSTEAD OF WORKERS

## Artificial Intelligence, Regulatory Friction, and the Conditions for Voluntary Relocalisation in the 21st Century


Ouadi Maakoul — June 2026
AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Qwen 

## ABSTRACT

Contemporary migration economics rests on a foundational spatial premise: labor moves to capital because capital cannot effectively move to labor. This dissertation challenges that premise through a formal multi-agent computational model. We ask whether artificial intelligence, digital telepresence, and emerging cross-border remote employment architectures are structurally eroding the spatial wage arbitrage that drives economic migration — and whether, under specific regulatory and technological conditions, voluntary relocalisation becomes a rational equilibrium at scale. We build and formally compare two 40-year trajectories (2025–2065): World A, in which current patterns of labor mobility continue under AI augmentation, and World B, in which AI-enabled progressive relocalisation occurs through infrastructure investment and bilateral treaty harmonization, not coercion. Four original theoretical contributions are made. First, a Revealed-Preference Origin Affinity Function that replaces the normatively-contaminated binary nationality construct with an empirically-estimable utility formulation. Second, a multi-layer network model distinguishing four edge types — Employment (EMP), Remittance (REM), Knowledge (KNW), and Social (SOC) — each with distinct type-specific telepresence preservation coefficients τ_T, yielding a Node/Edge Decomposition Theorem that generates opposite predictions under relocalisation for node and edge functions respectively. Third, a Regulatory Friction Coefficient ρ formally calibrated to the OECD's November 2025 Model Tax Convention update, modeled as an endogenous policy variable rather than a simplifying assumption. Fourth, a Political Feedback Model with an H-1B-payroll-weighted lobbying function and a Lobbying Decay Half-Life λ, producing a testable discontinuous prediction for immigration policy restrictiveness as a function of firm-level AI adoption. The dissertation focuses empirical attention on three core endogenous variables: Network Connectivity Index, Wage Convergence Rate, and Political Restriction Index. All remaining macroeconomic variables are treated as secondary derived outputs. Historical backcasting from 2010 to 2020 provides the model validation strategy.

