## CHAPTER 1 — INTRODUCTION

### 1.1 The Structural Question

Every year, approximately 281 million people live outside their country of birth. The demographic composition of that displacement is not accidental. It reflects a persistent structural asymmetry: the geography of high-productivity employment does not coincide with the geography of human populations, and physical migration is the mechanism by which individuals arbitrage that gap. A software engineer from Morocco earns approximately ten times more in Paris than in Casablanca. A registered nurse from the Philippines earns approximately eleven times more in California than in Manila. These are not marginal differentials. They are the dominant economic force shaping human geography in the 21st century.

This dissertation begins with a single structural question: what happens to that arbitrage when the job can move instead of the person?

The question was technologically unanswerable before 2020. Prior to the pandemic, the assumption that productive work required physical co-presence was empirically robust across the overwhelming majority of occupations. The pandemic falsified that assumption for knowledge work at a scale and speed that constituted an unprecedented natural experiment. The subsequent development of large language models and AI augmentation systems extended the falsification further. Eloundou, Manning, Mishkin, and Rock (2024) estimate that roughly 80% of U.S. workers have at least 10% of their tasks exposed to LLM assistance, and about 19% have more than 50% of duties affected — while emphasizing that "exposure" measures technical feasibility for task assistance, not a prediction of job destruction. [Laweconcenter](https://laweconcenter.org/resources/ai-productivity-and-labor-markets-a-review-of-the-empirical-evidence/)

We are now, for the first time in recorded economic history, in a period where it is technologically conceivable — though not yet economically routine — for a worker to provide full labor output to a high-wage-country firm while residing in a low-cost origin country. Deel's 2026 Global Hiring Report, analyzing over one million worker contracts across 150 countries, documents a 283% surge in cross-border AI trainer roles in 2025, with that single occupation now spanning 70,000+ workers across 600+ organizations. [Deel](https://www.deel.com/global-hiring-report-2026/) If this transition deepens structurally, the spatial logic that drives economic migration begins to unravel.

This dissertation does not argue that this transition will happen, nor that it is desirable if it does. It asks: under what conditions would it happen, through what mechanisms, and what are the second and third-order consequences that current policy frameworks are not equipped to model?

### 1.2 Why 2026 Is the Critical Window

Three converging developments make this the decisive moment for this inquiry.

First, AI capability has crossed a qualitative threshold in its labor market implications. The IMF estimates that approximately 40% of global employment faces meaningful AI exposure, with differential impacts across economic development levels: 60% in advanced economies, 40% in emerging markets, and 26% in low-income countries. [ResearchGate](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/397579321_The_Impact_of_Artificial_Intelligence_on_the_Future_Economy_Job_Displacement_and_Creation_2024-2030) This asymmetry has a non-obvious implication for migration economics: AI may initially widen effective wage differentials (by augmenting high-skill workers in advanced economies faster than in developing countries), temporarily increasing migration incentives, before the remote work channel subsequently narrows them.

Second, the political economy of immigration restriction is undergoing its most significant realignment since World War II. Amazon employed more than 14,000 H-1B holders as of mid-2025, with Microsoft, Meta, Apple, and Google each holding over 4,000 H-1B visas, placing them among the top ten recipients for fiscal year 2025. [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2025/09/20/trump-h-1b-visa-tech-foreign-governments.html) The Trump administration introduced a $100,000 fee on new H-1B applications — a policy that would have been politically inconceivable a decade ago, precisely because the employer lobby that prevented it has begun to weaken.

Third, the empirical natural experiment of pandemic-era remote work now provides calibration data that was not available to prior researchers. The 2020–2026 period constitutes a partially-controlled trial of the mechanisms our model formalizes.

### 1.3 The Return-to-Office Trend: A Clarification

A sharp reviewer will immediately note that the 2023–2025 period witnessed a pronounced Return-to-Office wave. If the dissertation argues that work is decoupling from location, does this empirical re-centralization falsify that claim?

No. The reason is theoretically significant. The RTO mandates were not driven by productivity failure of remote work. Bloom's randomized controlled trial at Trip.com, published in Nature in 2024, found that hybrid work — three days in office, two at home — had no impact on performance reviews, promotion rates, or lines of code written, and reduced quit rates by a third. [SIEPR](https://siepr.stanford.edu/av/Econ-To-Go-podcast-how-remote-work-redefining-our-lives) Bloom's global survey of 16,000+ college graduates across 40 countries found WFH levels have stabilized at 1.27 days per week in 2025, with 80% of Fortune 500 companies maintaining hybrid schedules — suggesting structural persistence, not reversal. [Stanford University](https://news.stanford.edu/stories/2025/04/remote-work-from-home-study-research-around-world)

The RTO mandates were driven primarily by the **principal-agent problem**: firms could not effectively verify remote worker output quality and effort without physical presence under pre-AI monitoring regimes. This is a monitoring problem, not a productivity problem. Generative AI and automated workflow tracking are independently solving the principal-agent problem of remote work. When AI can monitor, measure, and verify output continuously across geographic distances, the management rationale for physical co-presence dissolves. Current RTO mandates should therefore be interpreted as the final calibration of management practices to the pre-AI monitoring environment — a temporary friction consistent with Phase II transition dynamics — not as evidence of a stable re-centralization equilibrium.

### 1.4 Research Questions

RQ1 (Definitional): How should we formally define geographic belonging for individuals with multinational biographical identities, without making normative weight assignments?

RQ2 (Network): What is the structural effect of progressive relocalisation on the global economic network when migrants' productive (node) functions are distinguished from their connective (edge) functions, and when those edge functions are decomposed by type?

RQ3 (Comparative): Under what parameter conditions does World B produce higher or lower values than World A across the three core endogenous variables: Network Connectivity Index (NCI), Wage Convergence Rate (WCR), and Political Restriction Index (PRI)?

RQ4 (Political): Does AI adoption by technology firms endogenously remove the employer lobby that has historically constrained immigration restriction policy, and with what lag structure, as a function of H-1B-dependent payroll concentration?

### 1.5 Scope

This dissertation studies voluntary relocalisation driven by changed economic incentives. The analysis does not extend to forced deportation, ethnic nationalism, or any normative argument about cultural belonging. This is not a diplomatic qualification. It is a scientific necessity: the mechanisms, dynamics, and welfare consequences of economically-rational return migration are categorically different from those of coercive displacement. Any research design that conflates these two phenomena will generate structurally invalid results.

