## CHAPTER 2 — THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK

### 2.1 The Revealed-Preference Origin Affinity Function

The term "country of origin" conceals an irreducible normative problem that migration economics has characteristically avoided by assuming it away. One could define an Origin Affinity Vector:

**A_i** = (B_i, C_i, R_i, F_i, T_i, L_i, P_i)

where B_i = birth country, C_i = citizenship(s) held, R_i = residence history time-weighted, F_i = family network geographic distribution, T_i = cumulative time by country, L_i = primary language(s), P_i = self-reported identity preference. A score for country k could then be written:

O_i(k) = Σ_{j} [ w_j · φ_j(A_i, k) ]

where φ_j maps the j-th component of A_i to a [0,1] score for country k, and w_j are component weights satisfying Σ w_j = 1.

The critical problem is that the weight vector (w_1,...,w_7) is not empirically determinable. Different choices reflect different normative theories of belonging — blood-right assigns high w_B; residentialism assigns high w_R; subjectivism assigns high w_P — and no data can select among them. This is not a technical gap. It is an irreducible normative choice masquerading as a measurement question.

We therefore reformulate entirely. Rather than asking "where does person i belong" (which requires specifying weights), we ask "under what economic conditions does person i prefer to reside in country k" (which is empirically estimable from revealed preference data without weight specification). This reframing is not a retreat from rigor. It is an upgrade: it relocates the analysis from political philosophy into economics.

**Definition 1 (Residential Utility Function).** The utility to individual i of residing in country k at time t is:

U_i(k, t) = α · w_i(k, t) · (1 − ρ_i(k, t)) + β · q_i(k, t) + γ · s_i(k, t) + δ · ψ_i(k, t) − ε · d_i(k, t)

where:
- w_i(k, t) = expected gross wage accessible from country k at time t, determined by the agent's skill level and the AI-mediated remote earning capability
- ρ_i(k, t) ∈ [0,1] = Regulatory Friction Coefficient (defined in Section 2.3), which multiplies the wage term because regulatory friction reduces effective income but does not affect the quality of residential experience
- q_i(k, t) = quality-of-life composite index in k (health, education, safety, housing)
- s_i(k, t) = social network density in k
- ψ_i(k, t) = institutional quality index (rule of law, contract enforcement, political stability)
- d_i(k, t) = switching cost (psychological, financial, logistical) from current location to k

The individual's location choice is:

k_i*(t) = argmax_{k ∈ K_i} U_i(k, t)

where K_i is the set of countries feasibly accessible to agent i given citizenship and visa constraints.

**The AI intervention.** The key mechanism of the dissertation operates through w_i(k, t). In the pre-AI world, w_i(origin, t) is bounded by the local wage rate in the origin country, because the agent can only sell labor to origin-country employers. Under AI-mediated remote work, w_i(origin, t) approaches the global wage rate for the agent's skill level, discounted only by ρ_i and the type-specific telepresence preservation coefficient τ_T (defined in Chapter 4). When this discounted global wage exceeds the local host-country wage adjusted for cost of living, the utility inequality U_i(origin) > U_i(host) can be satisfied for the first time without requiring the agent to accept a wage cut. This is the core economic mechanism of voluntary relocalisation.

### 2.2 The Four-Phase Transition Model

**Phase I — Spatial Dependence (pre-2020).** Work requires physical co-presence for the overwhelming majority of productive tasks. Labor moves to capital. Migration is the dominant mechanism of global wage convergence. The telepresence preservation coefficient τ ≈ 0 for virtually all edge types.

**Phase II — Partial Decoupling (2020–2028, estimated).** Knowledge work decouples from physical location. Remote work normalizes but with management friction (the RTO wave). As of 2025, work-from-home days account for approximately 25% of all paid workdays in the U.S., stabilizing at a structurally elevated level above the pre-pandemic baseline. [Stanford University](https://news.stanford.edu/stories/2025/03/return-to-office-not-everybody-is-doing-it) Cross-border remote employment expands in knowledge-intensive occupations. The principal-agent problem of remote monitoring remains partially unresolved under pre-AI management systems.

**Phase III — Deep Decoupling (2028–2045, projected).** AI output verification resolves the principal-agent problem. AI augmentation extends decoupling into intermediate-skill work. Research from the Italian Institute of Technology published in May 2026 explores how telepresence robotics, AI, and low-latency connectivity can extend remote work into manufacturing, logistics, and hazardous industrial environments. [Robotics & Automation News](https://roboticsandautomationnews.com/2026/05/13/expert-opinion-extending-remote-work-to-the-factory-floor-through-telepresence/101491/) The wage differential w_i(origin) − w_i(host) net of cost-of-living differentials narrows substantially. Voluntary relocalisation becomes economically rational for a growing fraction of current migrants.

**Phase IV — New Equilibrium (post-2045).** A stable configuration emerges. Whether it resembles World A or World B depends on second-order factors including digital infrastructure quality in origin countries, pace of regulatory harmonization, social preferences for physical versus digital community, and the endogenous origin-country wage effect (Section 2.4).

Formally, let μ(t) denote global migration stock and τ̄(t) = Σ_T β_T τ_T(t) the weighted mean telepresence preservation coefficient. The migration dynamics equation is:

dμ/dt = f( Δw · (1 − ρ̄), Δq, Δs, τ̄, P_restrict )

In Phases I–II, dμ/dt > 0. In Phase III, as τ̄ → 1 and Δw · (1 − ρ̄) → 0, dμ/dt approaches zero or reverses sign. The dissertation's central computational task is estimating the parameter conditions under which this sign change occurs and the timeline over which it propagates.

### 2.3 The Regulatory Friction Coefficient

The previous draft version committed a critical error in modeling World B as "frictionless." This is not a simplification; it is a falsification. We correct it here.

**Definition 2 (Regulatory Friction Coefficient).** Let ρ_i(k, t) ∈ [0,1] represent the composite regulatory barrier to individual i earning wages from an employer located outside country k while residing in k, where ρ = 0 represents no regulatory barrier and ρ = 1 represents complete legal prohibition or full income confiscation by double taxation.

Three regulatory mechanisms constitute ρ:

**(a) Permanent Establishment (PE) Risk.** When an employee works remotely from a foreign country, their employer may inadvertently create a taxable corporate presence — a Permanent Establishment — in that country, triggering corporate income tax liability. The OECD's November 2025 update to the Model Tax Convention introduces a new analytical framework for PE risk in remote work scenarios: a 50% working time benchmark and a commercial reason test. No automatic PE arises from employee-driven remote work, but arrangements exceeding the 50% threshold require qualitative assessment of whether the employee's physical presence in the foreign country serves a genuine commercial purpose for the business. [EY](https://www.ey.com/en_ch/technical/tax-alerts/oecd-2025-update-new-rules-on-permanent-establishment-for-remote-work) The 50% benchmark provides a natural structural discontinuity in our ρ function: agents spending more than half their working time in their origin country face a qualitatively different regulatory environment than those below the threshold.

**(b) Social Security and Withholding Obligations.** Exceeding time thresholds can trigger social security affiliation reassignment, affecting contributions and coverage, particularly under multilateral agreements. European arrangements currently allow up to 49.9% telework between countries without changes to social security affiliation. [Ogletree](https://ogletree.com/insights-resources/blog-posts/cross-border-remote-work-and-permanent-establishment-mitigating-risk-for-multinational-employers/)

**(c) Data Localization.** GDPR Article 44, China's PIPL, and India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act create compliance barriers for firms employing distributed data workers across jurisdictions, particularly material for knowledge workers handling sensitive firm data.

**Modeling approach.** Rather than assuming ρ = 0 (the prior draft's error) or treating it as exogenous (the standard approach), we model ρ as an endogenous policy variable. In World A, ρ follows the observed trajectory of incremental OECD harmonization, declining slowly as bilateral treaty frameworks are updated. In World B, origin country State agents invest in negotiating bilateral Digital Employment Zone (DEZ) treaties — special regulatory frameworks reducing ρ through mutual PE exemptions, simplified withholding regimes, and data transfer agreements. World B is not a frictionless world. It is a world where friction is actively managed by origin country governments as a strategic development instrument.

The time evolution of ρ under World B is:

dρ_i(k,t)/dt = −κ · I_k(t) · DEZ_k(t)

where I_k(t) is the infrastructure investment rate of origin country k, DEZ_k(t) ∈ {0,1} indicates whether country k has an active bilateral DEZ treaty with destination country d, and κ is the treaty effectiveness coefficient estimated from existing bilateral frameworks.

### 2.4 The Origin Country Wage Effect: Boundary Condition

If World B succeeds and large-scale relocalisation occurs, what happens to wages in origin countries? The utility function in Definition 1 treats w_i(origin, t) as the global wage rate discounted by ρ and τ. This is correct for the first mover. It becomes incorrect at scale.

If a substantial fraction of a city's knowledge workers simultaneously access California-level wages while residing locally, the city's non-tradable sector — housing, food, domestic services, transportation — experiences a demand shock. Local price levels rise. The cost-of-living advantage that initially made origin country residence attractive is partially eroded endogenously. This is not merely an economic curiosity. It is the mechanism by which World B's equilibrium differs from a naive global wage equalization. The formally complete model requires:

q_i(origin, t) = q_i^0(origin) − φ · Ω(t)

where Ω(t) is the aggregate remote income inflow to origin country at time t and φ is the pass-through coefficient from remote income to local non-tradable prices. At low Ω(t), this term is negligible. At high Ω(t) — the Phase IV scenario — it becomes binding. We flag this as a critical second-order feedback that our first dissertation models as a sensitivity analysis boundary condition, and identify it explicitly as the priority extension for Phase IV dynamics.

