## CHAPTER 3 — LITERATURE REVIEW

### 3.1 Migration Economics and Its Foundational Limitation

The dominant analytical framework since Harris and Todaro (1970) treats labor movement as a utility-maximizing response to spatial wage differentials under uncertainty. Workers migrate when the expected discounted lifetime utility of residence in the destination country exceeds that of the origin, net of switching costs. This framework has been remarkably successful in explaining aggregate migration patterns for five decades. Its foundational limitation for our purposes is that it treats the wage differential as exogenous to technology: it has no mechanism by which AI could endogenize the differential by bringing wages to the worker rather than the worker to wages. This is not a small extension. It is a structural respecification of the model's ontology.

Subsequent contributions by Borjas (1987, 2003) on worker self-selection and Card (2001, 2022) on labor market impacts have substantially enriched the framework without challenging this foundational spatial assumption.

### 3.2 Brain Drain, Brain Gain, Brain Bank

The skilled migration welfare literature initiated by Bhagwati (1976) and substantially developed by Docquier and Rapoport (2012) has substantially qualified the simple brain drain narrative. Remittances constitute the most empirically robust positive transfer mechanism. According to the World Bank's Migration and Development Brief 38, officially recorded remittance flows to low- and middle-income countries reached $647 billion in 2022 and were projected to grow to $656 billion in 2023 — surpassing foreign direct investment and official development assistance as sources of external financing for developing countries. [ReliefWeb](https://reliefweb.int/report/world/migration-and-development-brief-38-remittances-remain-resilient-are-slowing-june-2023-enarjaruzh) By 2024, global remittance flows had reached an estimated $905 billion, reflecting continued structural resilience. [Migration data portal](https://www.migrationdataportal.org/themes/remittances-overview)

The "brain bank" concept (Agrawal, Kapur, McHale 2011) demonstrates that diaspora knowledge networks enable origin country firms to access research, technology, and investment leads even without the physical return of skilled emigrants. Our framework takes this one step further: under Phase III conditions, AI-enabled remote work transforms the brain bank from a diaspora mechanism into a domestic one. The engineer does not need to remain abroad to maintain productivity for a foreign employer. The metaphor collapses into the mechanism.

The empirical literature confirms that talents who migrate abroad contribute to their home economies through remittances, knowledge and technology transfer, and the formation of investment networks — providing empirical support for the brain bank channel that our model formalizes. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666188825006471)

### 3.3 Network Models of Migration

Massey et al. (1993) established the cumulative causation framework: each migration act generates social capital that lowers the cost of subsequent migration, creating self-reinforcing flows. This network-theoretic approach has been formalized by Munshi (2003) using social network methodology. These models treat migrants as nodes in social networks and examine how network structure shapes migration probability. They make an implicit assumption that has never been identified or challenged: that repatriation dissolves the edges connecting migrants to their host-country economic networks, i.e., that the telepresence preservation coefficient τ = 0 for all edge types.

Our model relaxes this assumption explicitly, allows τ to vary by edge type, and demonstrates that the predictions for relocalisation consequences differ qualitatively across edge types. This is not a marginal extension of existing network migration models. It changes the sign of a key predicted effect.

### 3.4 AI and Labor Markets

Research on AI and the labor market is, as a March 2026 PIIE review noted, "still in the first inning." Results are sensitive to the choice of AI exposure measure, and AI exposure may be correlated with other occupational characteristics including suitability for remote work and exposure to immigration — creating identification challenges that complicate causal inference. [PIIE](https://www.piie.com/blogs/realtime-economics/2026/research-ai-and-labor-market-still-first-inning) This is precisely the gap our joint model fills: by modeling AI augmentation and remote work as interacting mechanisms rather than independent variables, we avoid the confounding that afflicts single-mechanism studies.

Goldman Sachs estimates a 10-year timeline for wide-scale AI adoption, projecting 6–7% worker displacement during the transition period. [Goldman Sachs](https://www.goldmansachs.com/insights/articles/how-will-ai-affect-the-us-labor-market) This 10-year timeline is central to our political feedback model: the lobbying decay window is tightly bounded by how quickly AI adoption reduces the H-1B dependency of major technology firms.

### 3.5 Cross-Border Remote Work and Regulatory Friction

Mieriņa and Šūpule (2024), studying Latvia as a case of emigration-loss recovery through cross-border remote work, identify the potential of diaspora remote work to alleviate human capital losses — while identifying legal, tax, social benefit, and regulatory obstacles as significant barriers that require active policy intervention to overcome. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11107943/) This study is the empirically closest existing work to our World B mechanism. It predates the OECD's November 2025 PE guidance and does not formalize the friction coefficient endogenously. We extend their work on precisely these dimensions.

Research on remote work and spatial mobility in the EU confirms that remote work's influence on spatial patterns is more modest than initial post-pandemic predictions suggested, with evidence from Germany showing that working-from-home increased work-home distances but primarily within functional metropolitan labor markets rather than through long-distance relocation to remote regions. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.08252) This finding directly informs our τ calibration: the effective τ for cross-border relocalisation is lower than the τ for local remote work, requiring the discount factor adjustments formalized in Chapter 4.

