## CHAPTER 5 — THE POLITICAL FEEDBACK MECHANISM

### 5.1 The Historical Constraint on Immigration Restriction

Immigration restriction policies consistently poll favorably among native workers in every major high-income country. Yet they have historically failed to be implemented at the scale that polling would predict. The standard political economy explanation invokes the concentrated-versus-diffuse interests asymmetry (Olson 1965): firms employing migrant workers have strong, specific, financially material reasons to oppose restrictions; individual voters who prefer restrictions have weaker, general, non-monetized preferences. In the standard lobbying model, concentrated interests dominate diffuse preferences in policy outcomes.

The empirical evidence supports this explanation. With 65% of H-1B beneficiaries working in computer-related occupations in fiscal 2024, and with the GAMAM group of technology companies among the top eight H-1B employers, the technology sector has a direct and financially material stake in preserving the program's scale and cost structure. [statista](https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/7863/tech-company-h-1b-applications) The technology industry spent a record $85.6 million on federal lobbying in 2024, up from $68 million in 2023, with Meta ($24.2M), Amazon ($17.6M), Google ($12.1M), and Microsoft ($9.5M) as the leading spenders. [Axios](https://www.axios.com/pro/tech-policy/2025/01/22/2024-tech-lobbying-in-review)

A critical reviewer correctly identified that the aggregate lobbying figure includes spending on antitrust, privacy, content moderation, and other issues beyond immigration. The Λ(t) function must be calibrated to H-1B-specific advocacy, not total lobbying. We address this through H-1B-dependent payroll weighting, defined in Section 5.2.

### 5.2 The Political Feedback Formal Model

**Definition 7 (H-1B Payroll Dependency).** Define ω_F ∈ [0,1] as the fraction of firm F's total payroll cost attributable to H-1B-dependent employees. This quantity is estimable from USCIS approval data and firm-level payroll disclosures. Foreign-born employees represent approximately 19% of the U.S. STEM workforce overall, rising to 58% for doctoral computer scientists and 56% for doctoral engineers — concentrated precisely in the highest-wage positions that dominate payroll cost distributions at technology firms. [Northeastern Global News](https://news.northeastern.edu/2025/09/24/h1b-visa-fee-hike-startups/)

The H-1B-weighted lobbying expenditure for firm F is:

L_F^(H1B)(t) = L_F(t) · ω_F

This quantity represents the portion of firm F's total lobbying budget economically attributable to its H-1B dependency. It is the theoretically appropriate measure of employer opposition to immigration restriction, not total lobbying spend.

**Definition 8 (AI Substitution Threshold).** Let α_F(t) ∈ [0,1] denote firm F's AI adoption level at time t — the fraction of its current human-labor tasks that could be performed by available AI systems at equal or lower total cost. Let θ_F be a heterogeneous firm-specific threshold above which firm F becomes politically indifferent to or supportive of immigration restrictions.

The firm's immigration policy stance is:

ρ_F(t) = sign( θ_F − α_F(t) )

When α_F(t) < θ_F: firm actively opposes restriction (concentrated interest).
When α_F(t) > θ_F: firm is politically neutral or supportive (concentrated interest dissolves).

**Definition 9 (Aggregate Lobbying with Decay Half-Life).** Institutional inertia — long-term lobbying firm contracts, revolving-door dynamics, organizational momentum — means that a firm's political opposition does not cease the moment it crosses θ_F. We model the decay through a lobbying half-life parameter λ (measured in years):

Λ(t) = Σ_F [ max(0, θ_F − α_F(t)) · L_F^(H1B)(t) ] · e^{−λ · (t − t_F^θ)}

where t_F^θ is the time at which firm F crossed its threshold θ_F. The exponential decay term models the post-threshold institutional wind-down of lobbying activity.

**Calibration of λ.** The decay half-life λ is calibrated from historical analogues of concentrated interest dissolution following strategic reversals. The tobacco industry's lobbying program persisted for approximately 8–12 years after internal company documents demonstrated that executives privately accepted the health evidence against their public lobbying positions — a canonical case of institutional lobbying inertia. The financial services industry's lobbying against derivatives regulation persisted for approximately 6 years after internal risk management conclusions reversed. We use λ = 0.10 (corresponding to a half-life of approximately 7 years) as the baseline, with sensitivity analysis over λ ∈ [0.05, 0.20].

### 5.3 The Discontinuous PRI Prediction

Aggregate lobbying Λ(t) does not decline smoothly as AI adoption spreads. Firm threshold crossings θ_F are clustered around industry-level AI capability jumps (GPT-4 class, GPT-5 class, etc.). This clustering generates a discontinuous decline in Λ(t) at AI capability milestones rather than a smooth continuous decay.

The critical policy threshold is Λ*(t) — the minimum lobbying expenditure required to maintain immigration restriction policy at current levels. When Λ(t) < Λ*(t), the political constraint dissolves and restrictionist policy becomes achievable at the scale that public opinion has consistently demanded. This is a point prediction: there is a specific, calculable year at which PRI should increase discontinuously, determined by:

t_PRI = t_θ̄ + (1/λ) · ln( Λ_0 / Λ* )

where t_θ̄ is the mean firm threshold crossing time, Λ_0 is the lobbying expenditure at t_θ̄, and the logarithmic term captures the half-life decay period.

This prediction is falsifiable in real time against observed lobbying disclosure data (OpenSecrets.org, Center for Responsive Politics). Goldman Sachs' 10-year AI adoption timeline, combined with the current observation that immigration slowdowns are already contributing to measurable labor market shifts beginning in 2025, suggests the political threshold is being approached from both the technology side (increasing AI substitution) and the policy side (revealed political will to restrict) simultaneously. [Goldman Sachs](https://www.goldmansachs.com/insights/articles/how-will-ai-affect-the-us-labor-market)

### 5.4 Origin Country Second-Order Political Dynamics

A parallel political feedback operates on the origin country side. Countries that historically had incentives to facilitate emigration — relieving domestic unemployment, generating remittance inflows, exporting political pressure — face a reversal as destination country absorption capacity decreases. The educated domestic population that previously emigrated now remains present, underemployed, and politically active.

This generates a bifurcation at the origin country level. Governments respond with either: (a) constructive adaptation — infrastructure investment, regulatory reform to enable remote participation in the global economy, negotiation of DEZ treaties — generating World B dynamics; or (b) repression of the newly present frustrated educated class, generating political instability. We model this as a threshold function of origin country institutional quality ψ(k):

Response(k) = Constructive     if ψ(k) > ψ*
             = Repressive       if ψ(k) < ψ*

where ψ* is estimated from comparative historical cases of countries managing reversed emigration pressure. This threshold is testable against the post-2025 natural experiment of origin countries facing reduced destination-country absorption.

