## CHAPTER 6 — TESTABLE HYPOTHESES

**H1 (Network Integrity Threshold — from Corollary 1):** As τ̄ increases from 0 to 1, the NCI difference between World A and World B decreases monotonically. When τ_T > τ* for all T ∈ 𝒯, NCI(World B) = NCI(World A) ± ε. Identification: network simulation with τ_T varied as treatment variable across the empirically-calibrated range.

**H2 (Wage Convergence Differential):** WCR is higher in World B than World A across all four skill quartiles, with the largest World B premium in Q3 (intermediate-skilled workers) where AI augmentation and remote accessibility jointly apply. Identification: within-simulation difference-in-differences across skill quartiles, controlling for ρ and infrastructure investment.

**H3 (PRI Discontinuity):** PRI in destination countries increases discontinuously around the AI adoption threshold at which Λ(t) falls below Λ*, rather than as a smooth continuous increase. The discontinuity occurs at a calculable time t_PRI given by the formula in Section 5.3. Identification: structural break test on PRI time series; validated against observed 2025–2026 policy changes.

**H4 (Lobbying Decay Lag):** The timing of the PRI increase lags the AI adoption threshold crossing by approximately 1/λ years (7 years under baseline calibration). Identification: regression of PRI increase timing on firm-level α_F threshold crossing dates, weighted by ω_F.

**H5 (Institutional Bifurcation):** Origin countries with ψ(k) > ψ* respond constructively to reversed emigration pressure (increased DEZ treaty participation, infrastructure investment, measured reduction in ρ). Countries with ψ(k) < ψ* show increased political instability indices. Identification: post-2025 natural experiment with countries grouped by pre-2025 institutional quality.

**H6 (Regulatory Exclusion Inequality):** For origin countries not party to OECD Model Tax Convention frameworks — notably India (71% of 2024 H-1B approvals), China (12%), and much of Sub-Saharan Africa — elevated ρ values substantially reduce World B welfare gains relative to OECD-adjacent countries. This generates a new inequality dimension between treaty-connected and treaty-excluded developing economies. Identification: sensitivity analysis varying ρ across origin country institutional clusters.

**H7 (Agglomeration Lag):** Innovation rates (measured by patent output and startup formation) in World B lag World A for the first 10–15 years of the simulation, due to agglomeration effects (τ_KNW = 0.70, not 1.0; tacit knowledge losses persist). The World A innovation premium converges toward zero by the 20-year mark as distributed innovation infrastructure matures. Identification: secondary output of ABM simulation, not formally identified.

**H8 (Transition Path Dependence):** An abrupt World A → World B transition (simulated as a policy shock in year t = 0) produces statistically worse outcomes on NCI, WCR, and PRI relative to a gradual transition over 20+ years, holding steady-state parameters constant. Identification: comparison of shock versus gradual transition scenarios using identical endpoint parameters.

