## CHAPTER 7 — LIMITATIONS AND HONEST VULNERABILITIES

**L1 — Agglomeration Underestimation.** Our τ_KNW = 0.70 baseline acknowledges that physical proximity generates tacit knowledge spillovers that remote work cannot fully replicate. However, the empirical magnitude of the agglomeration premium in innovation production is contested in the literature (Audretsch and Feldman 1996 find strong effects; Bloom et al. 2024 find modest effects in hybrid settings). If the true agglomeration premium is at the high end of empirical estimates, World B innovation deficits in Hypothesis H7 will be larger and more persistent than our baseline predicts. We address this through τ_KNW sensitivity analysis over [0.50, 0.85].

**L2 — τ Cross-Border Calibration Uncertainty.** The discount factors δ_TZ, δ_CP, and δ_MT in Section 4.3 are calibrated from studies of same-country remote teams, not cross-border distributed teams. The gap between these empirical contexts introduces meaningful parameter uncertainty. The Deel contract-level data offers the best available source for granular cross-border productivity estimates, but it is not a controlled experiment. Confidence intervals on τ_EMP^(cross-border) are wide relative to other parameters.

**L3 — Non-OECD Regulatory Heterogeneity.** India's non-adoption of the OECD Model Tax Convention framework and its judiciary's distinct interpretive approach means that PE risk analysis requires country-specific treatment, significantly reducing the precision of a single ρ parameter for the largest single-country source population of economic migrants. [Lexology](https://www.lexology.com/library/detail.aspx?g=ffa56cb8-44cb-4561-951f-d8d4614e322f) Our ρ calibration is most robust for OECD-adjacent economies. For India, China, and Sub-Saharan African countries — collectively representing the largest migration source populations — ρ estimates carry substantially wider confidence intervals.

**L4 — Origin Country Wage Effect as Boundary Condition.** Section 2.4 identifies the endogenous erosion of origin country cost-of-living advantages as a critical Phase IV feedback that our first dissertation treats as a boundary condition. Any simulation that extends into Phase IV territory (post-2045) without this mechanism will overstate World B welfare gains for workers in origin countries. We bound this error by reporting Phase III results (2025–2045) as primary and Phase IV extensions as clearly labeled sensitivity scenarios.

**L5 — Identity Preference Dynamics.** The Residential Utility Function assumes stable preference weights (α, β, γ, δ, ε) over the simulation horizon. Empirical evidence suggests these weights shift across generations and in response to migration experience. Second-generation migrants' utility weights over origin country social network density may be structurally different from first-generation migrants'. We treat this as a boundary condition of the first dissertation and identify it as a priority extension.

