## CHAPTER 8 — CONTRIBUTIONS AND SIGNIFICANCE

**Contribution 1 — Revealed-Preference Origin Affinity Function.** Replaces the normatively-contaminated binary nationality construct in migration policy with a continuous utility-based formulation grounded in empirically-estimable parameters. Resolves the definitional incoherence that has corrupted migration policy debates by forcing the normative weight choices into explicit view rather than concealing them inside the definition of "origin."

**Contribution 2 — Multi-Layer Node/Edge Decomposition with τ_T-Specific Coefficients.** The first formal distinction between migrants' productive function (nodes in host-country clusters) and their connective function (edges between national subgraphs), with a four-type edge taxonomy (EMP, REM, KNW, SOC) each carrying empirically-calibrated type-specific telepresence preservation coefficients. The Node/Edge Decomposition Theorem formalizes why this distinction generates opposite predictions under relocalisation — the classical model (τ = 0) predicts near-complete network disintegration; the telepresence model (τ_REM = 0.95) predicts near-complete preservation of financial flows. These predictions are empirically distinguishable and falsifiable.

**Contribution 3 — Regulatory Friction Endogenization.** The first migration economics model treating the Permanent Establishment coefficient as an endogenous policy variable, formally calibrated to the OECD's November 2025 Model Tax Convention update. This provides a framework for evaluating the welfare effects of bilateral Digital Employment Zone treaties and generates precise, welfare-quantified predictions for the policy value of regulatory harmonization.

**Contribution 4 — Political Feedback Model with H-1B-Weighted Lobbying Decay.** The first formal model of the mechanism by which AI adoption removes the employer lobby constraining immigration restriction policy. The H-1B-payroll-weighted lobbying function Λ(t) corrects the aggregate lobbying figures to isolate immigration-relevant advocacy. The decay half-life λ captures institutional inertia. Together they generate a falsifiable point prediction for the year in which PRI should increase discontinuously, derivable from currently observable lobbying disclosure data and AI adoption indices.

