## CONCLUSION

This dissertation proposes to study whether artificial intelligence is dissolving the spatial wage arbitrage that has made migration the dominant mechanism of global wage convergence since industrialization. The question is not ideological. It has precise, testable implications for three observable variables — network connectivity, wage convergence, and political restriction — all of which are currently generating real-world data against which the model can be validated.

The model will be wrong in specific ways enumerated honestly in Chapter 7. But it will generate falsifiable predictions derived from first-principle formalism, calibrated to publicly verifiable data sources, and organized around a backcasting validation requirement that prevents post-hoc specification. That is the standard for scientific inquiry, and it is the only standard that should be applied.

Whether the answer to the central question is optimistic or pessimistic about human mobility, the answer matters. It will determine, to a significant degree, the political economy of immigration restriction for the next generation. That is why a rigorous, non-normative model is more valuable than a well-intentioned one.

