APPENDIX F — CALIBRATION METHODOLOGY AND DATA SOURCES (EXPANDED)

F.1 τ Coefficients

· τ_EMP^(hybrid) = 0.65: from Bloom et al. (2024) RCT at Trip.com, where hybrid workers (3 days office, 2 home) maintained identical performance metrics. This is a conservative lower bound for knowledge workers.
· τ_EMP^(cross-border): derived from hybrid baseline with multiplicative discounts:
  · δ_TZ: Timezone friction. Fitted using a quadratic function of absolute time-zone difference $\Delta TZ$: $\delta_{TZ} = \min(0.3, a (\Delta TZ)^2 + b \Delta TZ)$. Coefficients estimated from Gibbs et al. (2021) remote team productivity regressions. For Morocco–France (ΔTZ=0, but CET vs WET maybe 1 hour) δ_TZ ≈ 0.02; for Philippines–US West Coast (ΔTZ=15h) δ_TZ≈0.18.
  · δ_CP: Cultural proximity. Constructed from WALS linguistic distance and Hofstede cultural dimensions. We compute a composite distance metric and map to [0,0.2] linearly.
  · δ_MT: Management trust. At α_F=0, δ_MT=δ_MT0=0.25; declines to 0 as α_F→1. Calibrated from survey data on managerial resistance to full remote.
    Typical cross-border τ_EMP for an Indian software engineer working for a US tech firm with AI monitoring (α_F=0.7) and 12.5h TZ difference: τ_EMP = 0.65 * (1-0.15) * (1-0.10) * (1-0.075) ≈ 0.65 * 0.85 * 0.90 * 0.925 ≈ 0.46.
· τ_REM = 0.95: Based on observed persistence of remittance flows when migrants return, e.g., Philippine returnees continue sending some remittances initially; we set a small decay. World Bank data on return migration corridors shows remittances drop by only 5-10% in the first five years after return.
· τ_KNW = 0.70: Derived from studies of diaspora knowledge networks; e.g., Agrawal et al. (2011) find that patent citations remain at 70% of the co-located level when inventors are distant but connected electronically. Tacit knowledge loss estimated at 30%.
· τ_SOC = 0.50: Based on Dunbar’s number and Bailenson (2021) on Zoom fatigue; social tie strength decays substantially without physical co-presence.

F.2 Switching Costs

Following Kennan and Walker (2011) and migration cost estimates, switching costs are drawn from a log-normal distribution with mean equivalent to 50% of annual income, with a standard deviation of 20%. This is reduced to 8% for hybrid moves (Bloom et al.) but for full cross-border relocation, higher.

F.3 AI Adoption Rates

Adoption follows a Bass diffusion model with parameters based on Goldman Sachs (2023) forecasts: innovation coefficient p = 0.01, imitation coefficient q = 0.3, total market saturation by 2045. The AI capability index A(t) grows logistically.

F.4 Lobbying Parameters

· H-1B payroll dependency ω_F: estimated from USCIS LCA data and firm 10-K filings. For top tech firms, ω ranges from 0.08 to 0.22.
· Threshold θ_F: based on the fraction of tasks automatable by AI. We set θ_F ~ 0.5 for most tech firms, meaning when half their current H-1B task set can be automated, they become indifferent.
· Decay half-life λ: set at 7 years (λ = ln2 / 7 ≈ 0.099). Sensitivity 4–10 years.

F.5 Network Initialization

The initial network is built from actual bilateral migration stock data (UN DESA 2020) and remittance corridors (World Bank). We sample 100,000 agents stratified by corridor, assign skills and edges probabilistically to match aggregate remittance volume, trade, and patent co-authorship statistics.

APPENDIX G — BACKCASTING VALIDATION PROTOCOL

The model is run from 2010 to 2020 with pre-AI parameters:

· AI capability = 0 (no meaningful AI augmentation).
· τ_EMP^(cross-border) = 0.10 (very limited remote cross-border work).
· ρ reflects pre-OECD 2025 regulatory environment with high uncertainty.
· Switching costs high, no DEZ treaties, low origin-country infrastructure.

Validation metrics:

1. Global migrant stock growth: Model must produce a stock in 2020 of 281 ± 15% million.
2. Bilateral corridor shares: For top 20 corridors, predicted shares must correlate with actual shares (Pearson r > 0.85).
3. Wage ratio stability: In the absence of remote work, bilateral wage ratios for major corridors (e.g., Mexico–US, India–US, Philippines–US) should remain within 10% of 2010 values, reflecting the classic persistent wage gap.

If the model fails, we recalibrate migration propensity parameters (utility weights, switching cost distribution) until it reproduces these facts, then fix them for forward simulations.

APPENDIX H — ORIGIN COUNTRY WAGE EFFECT: FORMAL MODEL

H.1 Aggregate Remote Income Inflow

Define $\Omega_k(t) = \sum_{i \in \text{residing in } k} w_i^{\text{remote}}(t)$, where $w_i^{\text{remote}}$ is the portion of income earned from abroad. This is a function of the number of relocalised workers and their wages. The effect on non-tradable prices is modeled via a housing/services demand shock. Let $q_{k}(t) = q_k^0 - \phi \cdot \Omega_k(t)/\text{population}_k$, where $\phi$ is the passthrough coefficient (estimated from urban economics literature: 0.3–0.6). The quality-of-life index also includes congestion effects. We incorporate this in Phase IV simulations only, by making $q_k$ endogenous in the utility function. For Phase III (2025–2045), $\Omega_k$ is small enough that the effect is negligible (less than 2% erosion), verified by ABM.

